    {
        "id": "d5d19656df4b3761",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "89462d0157a97648",
        "g": "2550bfa94693154d",
        "name": "Notes on handling PowerMeasurement",
        "info": "The `Fake PowerMeasurement` node below always sends the power measurements which are expected by the currently active operation mode. This, of course, does not accurately and reliably reflect the real world and can therefore cause Opportunity Loads to not work well.\n\nWhen this is expected to work okay:\n- If this is the only device (next to the battery) that is controlled by Opportunity Loads\n- If the device has a very stable power usage and therefore the configured values in the OMBC System Description closely match the reality\n\nEspecially if the power usage of the device is not stable, it is highly recommended to replace the `Fake PowerMeasurement` by providing actual power  measurements from the device. Those can come from a Victron energy meter, Shelly device or any other source.\n\n### Example for when actual power measurements are recommended\n\nWhen using the Cerbo internal relay to control the SG Ready \"Boost-mode\" of a heat pump; The heat pump will still follow its internal logic to produce heat for a certain comfort level, even if the relay is off. And it might not start or stop after a while to consume extra power even if the relay is still on.",
        "x": 210,
        "y": 580,
        "wires": []
    },
